      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-288639",
      ":question/problem": "Consider the equation in the variable $v,$ given by\n\n$$\n2(q-7)v+5=1\n$$\n\nwhere $q$ is a constant. Given that this equation has no solution, what is the value of $q?$\n\n$q=$\n{{field-1}}",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\ne 0.$ If $a=0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nFirst, let's write our equation in the form $av=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(q-7)v+5 & =1 \\\\ 2(q-7)v+5-5 & =1-5 \\\\ {{2(q-7)}_{\u23df}}_{a}v & ={{-4}_{\u23df}}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\n2(q-7)=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $q,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(q-7) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{2(q-7)}{2} & =\\frac{0}{2} \\\\ q-7 & =0 \\\\ q & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$"
